course: dictionary
lesson: 8010
title: Reefs: rock, sand and coral under the sea
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A reef is a long ridge of rock, sand or coral that sits just under the sea or reaches up to the surface. When you meet the word reef in a text, read the words around it: they are its context, and they leave clues.
explain: «Newly hatched clownfish drift in the open sea as tiny larvae for about one or two weeks, then find a reef by its smell and crackling sound and swim to it.» The fish swim to the reef and use its sound and smell to find it, so a reef must be a place where fish live. Try the meaning in the sentence: a place in the sea where fish gather still makes sense.
explain: «The stonefish looks exactly like a lumpy stone and has venomous spines along its back, so reef shoes in shallow water protect your feet.» Shallow water and protect your feet tell you that a reef is near the surface, where people walk or swim, and that it can hurt bare feet.
fact: A reef is a long ridge of rock, sand or coral just under or at the surface of the sea.
fact: A coral reef is built over many years by tiny sea animals called corals, and it is home to huge numbers of fish and other sea creatures.
fact: The Great Barrier Reef off Australia is the largest coral reef system in the world and can be seen from space.
fact: Warmer, more acidic seas can cause «coral bleaching», when corals turn white and may die.
fact: Reef is also a sailing word: to reef a sail means to roll or tie up part of it to make it smaller in strong wind.
exercise: «Newly hatched clownfish ... find a reef by its smell and crackling sound.» What is a reef? | A sail that is rolled up on a boat | *A place in the sea where fish and sea creatures live | A large wave that breaks on the beach | A type of fish that swells into a spiky ball
exercise: «The stonefish looks exactly like a lumpy stone ... reef shoes in shallow water protect your feet.» Why do people wear reef shoes? | To swim faster than the fish | To catch clownfish | To hide from the sun | *To protect their feet from sharp spines and rocks in shallow water
exercise: In a science lesson, you read that «coral bleaching» can happen in warmer, more acidic seas. What happens to the corals? | Corals swim away to cooler water | *Corals turn white and may die | Fish turn into stone | Corals grow very tall overnight
test: The sailing sense: to reef a sail means what? | To paint it bright colours | To lose it in the sea | To stretch it as wide as possible in calm air | *To roll or tie up part of it to make it smaller in strong wind
test: Which word is a good synonym for reef in the sea sense? | *Ridge | Puddle | Cloud | Island
exam: Which place is the largest coral reef system in the world? | Lake Superior | *The Great Barrier Reef off Australia | Mount Everest | The Sahara Desert
